<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Urban II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1035–<a href="/years/1099">1099</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Urban II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Urban II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 1088 to 1099. In 1095, responding to an appeal from the Byzantine emperor Alexios I framed around the Seljuk threat, he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> that followed were partly a consequence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>'s advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Urban II" width="308" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urbano_II_in_consacrazione_de_l%27altare_del_monastero_di_Cluny_(detail)_(crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Urban II was baptised Eudes, or Odo, and was born into a noble family of Châtillon-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1050 he began his studies at the cathedral school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. He became prior of the abbey of Cluny, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> named him cardinal-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ostia in about 1080. He was a prominent supporter of the Gregorian reforms, especially as legate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1084. Odo was elected by acclamation at a small meeting of cardinals and other prelates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracina">Terracina</a> in March 1088, after the short reign of Victor III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From the start Urban faced Guibert, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ravenna, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as the antipope Clement III after the Holy Roman Emperor Henry IV took the city in 1084. Urban continued Gregory's policies with more flexibility and diplomacy. Kept away from Rome, he toured northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and synods renewed condemnations of simony, lay investiture and clerical marriage. He upheld the excommunication of King Philip I of France over his marriage to Bertrade de Montfort.</p><p>At the Council of Piacenza in March 1095 he received an ambassador from the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> asking for help against the Turkish tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a>, on 27 November 1095, he preached in the open air to a large crowd and called on them to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> and the eastern churches from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. He promised remission of sins to those who went, and the same indulgence applied to fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>No exact record of the Clermont speech exists. Five versions survive, written later and differing widely; Fulcher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>, who was present, wrote his in about 1101. Urban's own letters speak more of liberating the eastern churches than of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Historians disagree about his motives: some point to reunion with the Eastern Church after the schism of 1054, others to his need for legitimacy against Clement III, others to fear of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>.</p><p>French armies removed Clement III from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1097. Urban died on 29 July 1099, fourteen days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> fell but before the news reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Paschal II succeeded him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a> beatified him on 14 July 1881.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Urban II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Toulouse itself was dominated by the great Romanesque basilica of Saint-Sernin, whose altar <b>Pope Urban II</b> consecrated in 1096.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Jerusalem was created by the First Crusade, launched after <b>Pope Urban II</b>’s appeal at Clermont in 1095.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Manzikert opened Asia Minor to Turkmen settlement and in time helped precipitate the Crusades, as Emperor Alexios I's appeal to <b>Pope Urban II</b> in 1095 was framed around the Turkish threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Urban II</b> elevated the Diocese of Pisa, founded in the 4th century, to the dignity of a metropolitan archdiocese, with its seat in the cathedral of the Assumption.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Raniero Raineri di Bleda, a monk of Cluny, was elected pope as Paschal II after the death of <b>Pope Urban II</b> the previous month.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Elected at Terracina while a rival pope held Rome, <b>Urban II</b> continued Gregory VII's reforms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1076" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Pope Urban II"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1076">What was here in 1076? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let those who for a long time, have been robbers, now become knights. Let those who have been fighting against their brothers and relatives now fight in a proper way against the barbarians.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Fulcher of Chartres' version) &#183; tr. Oliver J. Thatcher and Edgar H. McNeal, 1905 &#183; the speech as Fulcher of Chartres, who was there, recorded it &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When an armed attack is made upon the enemy, let this one cry be raised by all the soldiers of God: It is the will of God! It is the will of God!&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Robert the Monk's version) &#183; tr. Dana C. Munro, 1895 &#183; the speech as Robert the Monk, who was there, retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Oh, most valiant soldiers and descendants of invincible ancestors, be not degenerate, but recall the valor of your progenitors.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Robert the Monk's version) &#183; tr. Dana C. Munro, 1895 &#183; the speech as Robert the Monk, who was there, retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You, girt about with the badge of knighthood, are arrogant with great pride; you rage against your brothers and cut each other in pieces. This is not the (true) soldiery of Christ which rends asunder the sheepfold of the Redeemer.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Balderic of Dol's version) &#183; tr. August C. Krey, 1921 &#183; the speech as Balderic of Dol retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Short is the way, little the labor, which, nevertheless, will repay you with the crown that fadeth not away.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech at the Council of Clermont, 1095 (Balderic of Dol's version) &#183; tr. August C. Krey, 1921 &#183; the speech as Balderic of Dol retold it some years later &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/urban2-5vers.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
